<item>
  <title>What am I doing?</title>
  <link>https://your-website-url.example.com/posts/What am I doing?/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>I’ve been lucky to work with, meet and study under some very talented data professionals and academics across Scotland. And, after some minor sleuthing of their GitHubs and LinkedIns, I’ve noticed something: at some point in their career, they start a blog.</p>
<p>Some are long abandoned with HTML left stale and rotting. Others are active in the form of a weak, irregular pulse of site updates and code snippets. In the hopes of following in their footsteps, I am – quite literally – following in their footsteps with this hastily put together GitHub Pages mini-website. The aim is equal parts showcase and journal of professional milestones, undercooked opinions and code I’ve used across work/personal projects. To that end, I guess the first piece to show off is this website!</p>
<p>Working in the R/tidyverse space for a while now has been very rewarding: I’ve always had a tech slant to my hobbies and interests but my technical proficiency has really deepened over the last twelve months. One project I’m particularly proud of was a prototype R Shiny dashboard centered on the idea of “streaks” that I finished before summer. It was a completely new way of looking at the data in our programme; shifting away from the monthly reporting of performance data and more towards a focus on long-term - much more difficult to sustain - trends. I ended up re-writing everything in SQL to take advantage of the newly available database access my team had secured and save on local processing at the same time.</p>
<section id="calculating-performance-streaks-in-sql" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="calculating-performance-streaks-in-sql">Calculating performance streaks in SQL</h2>
<p>The “streaks” calculation has a few parts. It starts by simply setting up the first of four CTEs in SQL, <code>meta_data</code>, a table of reference values for each performance indicator.</p>
